L32:       3. cite22†4.3 Metastability and a phase transition L33:     2. cite23†5 A single cluster for small $\\beta$ L34:       1. cite24†5.1 The case $\\beta=0$ L35:       2. cite25†5.2 The case $\\beta\\ll 1$ L36:   5. cite26†III Further questions L37:     1. cite27†6 Dynamics on the circle L38:       1. cite28†6.1 Angular equations L39:       2. cite29†6.2 The Kuramoto model L40:     2. cite30†7 General matrices L41:       1. cite31†7.1 The repulsive case L42:       2. cite32†7.2 Pure self-attention L43:         1. cite33†7.2.1 A review of [GLPR23] L44:         2. cite34†7.2.2 Singular dynamics L45:     3. cite35†8 Approximation and control L46:   6. cite36†A Proof of Theorem  L47:   7. cite37†B Proof of Theorem  L48:   8. cite38†C Proof of Proposition L49:     1. cite39†C.1 Proof of Remark L50:   9. cite40†References L51: cite41†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L52: \nL53: arXiv:2312.10794v2 [cs.LG] 22 Dec 2023\nL54: # A mathematical perspective on Transformers\nL55: Borjan Geshkovski Address: Department of Mathematics, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, 77 Massachusetts Ave, 02139 Cambridge MA, USA Email address: borjan@mit.edu , Cyril Letrouit Address: CNRS & Université Paris-Saclay\nL56: Laboratoire de mathématiques d’Orsay, 307 rue Michel Magat, Bâtiment 307, 91400 Orsay, France Email address: cyril.letrouit@universite-paris-saclay.fr , Yury Polyanskiy Address: Department of EECS, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, 77 Massachusetts Ave, 02139 Cambridge MA, USA Email address: yp@mit.edu and Philippe Rigollet Address: Department of Mathematics, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, 77 Massachusetts Ave, 02139 Cambridge MA, USA Email address: rigollet@math.mit.edu\nL57: ###### Abstract.\nL58: \nL59: Transformers play a central role in the inner workings of large language models. We develop a mathematical framework for analyzing Transformers based on their interpretation as interacting particle systems, which reveals that clusters emerge in long time. Our study explores the underlying theory and offers new perspectives for mathematicians as well as computer scientists.\nL60: ###### Key words and phrases:\nL61: \nL62: Transformers, self-attention, interacting particle systems, clustering, gradient flows\nL63: \nL64: ###### 1991 Mathematics Subject Classification\nL65: \nL66: Primary: 34D05, 34D06, 35Q83; Secondary: 52C17\nL67: ###### Contents\nL68:   1. cite6†1 Outline L69:   2. cite7†I Modeling L70:     1. cite8†2 Interacting particle system L71:     2. cite14†3 Measure to measure flow map L72:   3. cite18†II Clustering L73:     1. cite19†4 A single cluster in high dimension L74:     2. cite23†5 A single cluster for small $\\beta$ L75:   4. cite26†III Further questions L76:     1. cite27†6 Dynamics on the circle L77:     2. cite30†7 General matrices L78:     3. cite35†8 Approximation and control L79:   5. cite36†A Proof of Theorem  L80:   6. cite37†B Proof of Theorem  L81:   7. cite38†C Proof of Proposition L82:   8. cite40†References L83: ### 1. Outline\nL84: The introduction of Transformers in 2017 by Vaswani et al. [cite42†VSP+17 ] marked a significant milestone in development of neural network architectures. Central to this contribution is self-attention, a novel mechanism which distinguishes Transformers from traditional architectures, and which plays a substantial role in their superior practical performance.\nL85: In fact, this innovation has been a key catalyst for the progress of artificial intelligence in areas such as computer vision and natural language processing, notably with the emergence of large language models. As a result, understanding the mechanisms by which Transformers, and especially self-attention, process data is a crucial yet largely uncharted research area.\nL86: A common characteristic of deep neural networks (DNNs) is their compositional nature: data is processed sequentially, layer by layer, resulting in a discrete-time dynamical system (we refer the reader to the textbook [cite43†GBC16 ] for a general introduction). This perspective has been successfully employed to model residual neural networks—see Section cite9†2.1 for more details—as continuous-time dynamical systems called neural ordinary differential equations (neural ODEs) [cite44†CRBD18 , cite45†E17 , cite46†HR17 ].\nL87: In this context, an input $x(0)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, say an image, is evolving according to a given time-varying velocity field as $\\dot{x}(t)=v_{t}(x(t))$ over some time interval $(0,T)$. As such, a DNN can be seen as a flow map $x(0)\\mapsto x(T)$ from $\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ to $\\mathbb{R}^{d}$.\nL88: Even within the restricted class of velocity fields $\\{v_{t}\\}_{t\\geq 0}$ imposed by classical DNN architectures, such flow maps enjoy strong approximation properties as exemplified by a long line of work on these questions [cite47†LJ18 , cite48†ZGUA20 , cite49†LLS22 , cite50†TG22 , cite51†RBZ23 , cite52†CLLS23 ].\nL89: In this article we observe that Transformers are in fact flow maps on $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^{d})$, the space of probability measures over $\\mathbb{R}^{d}$. To realize this flow map from measures to measures, Transformers evolve a mean-field interacting particle system. More specifically, every particle (called a token in this context) follows the flow of a vector field which depends on the empirical measure of all particles.\nL90: In turn, the continuity equation governs the evolution of the empirical measure of particles, whose long-time behavior is of crucial interest. In this regard, our main observation is that particles tend to cluster under these dynamics. This phenomenon is of particular relevance in learning tasks such as next-token prediction, wherein one seeks to map a given input sequence (i.e., a sentence) of $n$ tokens (i.e., words) onto a given next token.\nL91: In this case, the output measure encodes the probability distribution of the next token, and its clustering indicates a small number of possible outcomes. Our results indicate that the limiting distribution is actually a point mass, leaving no room for diversity or randomness, which is at odds with practical observations. This apparent paradox is resolved by the existence of a long-time metastable state.\nL92: As can be seen from Figures cite53†2 and cite54†4 , the Transformer flow appears to possess two different time-scales: in a first phase, tokens quickly form a few clusters, while in a second (much slower) phase, through the process of pairwise merging of clusters, all tokens finally collapse to a single point.\nL93: The goal of this manuscript is twofold. On the one hand, we aim to provide a general and accessible framework to study Transformers from a mathematical perspective. In particular, the structure of these interacting particle systems allows one to draw concrete connections to established topics in mathematics, including nonlinear transport equations, Wasserstein gradient flows, collective behavior models, and optimal configurations of points on spheres, among others.\nL94: On the other hand, we describe several promising research directions with a particular focus on the long-time clustering phenomenon. The main results we present are new, and we also provide what we believe are interesting open problems throughout the paper.\nL95: The rest of the paper is arranged in three parts.\nL96: ##### Part cite7†I : Modeling\nL97: We define an idealized model of the Transformer architecture that consists in viewing the discrete layer indices as a continuous time variable. This abstraction is not new and parallels the one employed in classical architectures such as ResNets [cite44†CRBD18 , cite45†E17 , cite46†HR17 ]. Our model focuses exclusively on two key components of the Transformers architecture: self-attention and layer-normalization.\nL98: Layer-normalization effectively constrains particles to evolve on the unit sphere $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$, whereas self-attention is the particular nonlinear coupling of the particles done through the empirical measure. (Section cite8†2 ). In turn, the empirical measure evolves according to the continuity partial differential equation (Section cite14†3 ).\nL99: We also introduce a simpler surrogate model for self-attention which has the convenient property of being a Wasserstein gradient flow [cite55†AGS05 ] for an energy functional that is well-studied in the context of optimal configurations of points on the sphere.\nL100: ##### Part cite18†II : Clustering\nL101: In this part we establish new mathematical results that indicate clustering of tokens in the large time limit. Our main result, Theorem cite56†4.1 , indicates that in high dimension $d\\geq n$, a set of $n$ particles randomly initialized on $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ will cluster to a single point as $t\\to+\\infty$. We complement this result with a precise characterization of the rate of contraction of particles into a cluster.\nL102: Namely, we describe the histogram of all inter-particle distances, and the time at which all particles are already nearly clustered (Section  cite19†4 ). We also obtain a clustering result without assuming that the dimension $d$ is large, in another asymptotic regime (Section cite23†5 ).\nL103: ##### Part cite26†III : Further questions\nL104: We propose potential avenues for future research, largely in the form of open questions substantiated by numerical observations. We first focus on the case $d=2$ (Section cite27†6 ) and elicit a link to Kuramoto oscillators. We briefly show in Section cite31†7.1 how a simple and natural modification of our model leads to non-trivial questions related to optimal configurations on the sphere.\nL105: The remaining sections explore interacting particle systems that allow for parameter tuning of the Transformers architectures, a key feature of practical implementations.\nL106: ## Part I Modeling\nL107: We begin our discussion by presenting the mathematical model for a Transformer (Section cite8†2 ). We focus on a slightly simplified version that includes the self-attention mechanism as well as layer normalization, but excludes additional feed-forward layers commonly used in practice; see Section cite13†2.3.2 . This leads to a highly nonlinear mean-field interacting particle system.\nL108: In turn, this system implements, via the continuity equation, a flow map from initial to terminal distributions of particles that we present in Section cite14†3 .\nL109: ### 2. Interacting particle system\nL110: \nL111: Before writing down the Transformer model, we first provide a brief preliminary discussion to clarify our methodological choice of treating the discrete layer indices in the model as a continuous time variable in Section cite9†2.1 , echoing previous work on ResNets. The specifics of the Transformer model are presented in Section cite10†2.2 .\nL112: #### 2.1. Residual neural networks\nL113: One of the standard paradigms in machine learning is that of supervised learning, where one aims to approximate an unknown function $f:\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\to\\mathbb{R}^{m}$, from data, ${D}=\\{x^{(i)},f(x^{(i)})\\}_{i\\in[N]}$ say. This is typically done by choosing one among an arsenal of possible parametric models, whose parameters are then fit to the data by means of minimizing some user-specified cost.\nL114: With the advent of graphical processing units (GPUs) in the realm of computer vision [cite57†KSH12 ], large neural networks have become computationally accessible, resulting in their popularity as one such parametric model.\nL115: Within the class of neural networks, residual neural networks (ResNets for short) have become a staple DNN architecture since their introduction in [cite58†HZRS16 ]. In their most basic form, ResNets approximate a function $f$ at $x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ through a sequence of affine transformations, a component-wise nonlinearity, and skip connections. Put in formulae,\nL116: \nL117: (2.1)  |  | $$\\begin{cases}x(k+1)=x(k)+w(k)\\sigma(a(k)x(k)+b(k))&\\text{ for }k\\in\\{0,\\ldots,L-1\\}\\\\\nL118: x(0)=x\\,.\\end{cases}$$  |\nL119: Here $\\sigma$ is a Lipschitz function applied component-wise to the input vector, while $\\theta(\\cdot)=(w(\\cdot),a(\\cdot),b(\\cdot))\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ are trainable parameters. We say that (cite59†2.1 ) has $L\\geq 1$ hidden layers (or $L+1$ layers, or is of depth $L$).\nL120: The output of the ResNet given the $i$-th input, namely $x_{i}(L)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, is projected to $\\mathbb{R}^{m}$ via a trained transformation as to match the label $f(x^{(i)})$ according to the user-specified objective. One can also devise generalizations of (cite59†2.1 ), for instance in which matrix-vector multiplications are replaced by discrete convolutions.\nL121: The key element that all these models share is that they all have skip-connections, namely, the previous step $x_{i}(k)$ appears explicitly in the iteration for the next one.\nL122: One upside of (cite59†2.1 ), which is the one of interest to our narrative, is that the layer index $k$ can naturally be interpreted as a time variable, motivating the continuous-time analogue\nL123: \nL124: (2.2)  |  | $$\\begin{cases}\\dot{x}(t)=w(t)\\sigma(a(t)x(t)+b(t))&\\text{ for }t\\in(0,T)\\\\\nL125: x(0)=x.\\end{cases}$$  |\nL126: These are dubbed neural ordinary differential equations (neural ODEs). Since their introduction in [cite44†CRBD18 , cite45†E17 , cite46†HR17 ], neural ODEs have emerged as a flexible mathematical framework to implement and study ResNets.\nL127: #### 2.2. The interacting particle system\nL128: Unlike ResNets, which operate on a single input vector $x(0)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ at a time, Transformers operate on a sequence of vectors of length $n$, namely, $(x_{i}(0))_{i\\in[n]}\\in(\\mathbb{R}^{d})^{n}$. This perspective is rooted in natural language processing, where each vector represents a word, and the entire sequence a sentence or a paragraph. In particular, it allows to process words together with their context.\nL129: A sequence element $x_{i}(0)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ is called a token, and the entire sequence $(x_{i}(0))_{i\\in[n]}$ a prompt. We use the words “token” and “particle” interchangeably.\nL130: Practical implementations make use of layer normalization [cite60†BKH16 ], which amounts to an element-wise standardization of every particle at every layer.\nL131: This effectively constrains particles to evolve on a time-varying axis-aligned ellipsoid, that we take to be the unit sphere $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ in the rest of this paper.^{1}^{1} 1 Layer normalization originally consisted in an entry-wise standardization of every token and a skew via a trained matrix at every layer, leading to said axis-aligned ellipsoid.\nL132: However, different practical implementations use different but related variants, all with the goal of ensuring that tokens don’t diverge as to avoid rounding errors. Considering the unit sphere is thus a reasonable and natural modeling choice. This is even verified empirically in the pre-trained ALBERT XLarge v2 model described in Figure cite61†1 , and used explicitly in Mistral AI’s model ( cite62†https://github.com/mistralai/mistral-src/tree/main†github.com ).\nL133: A Transformer is then a flow map on $(\\mathbb{S}^{d-1})^{n}$: the input sequence $(x_{i}(0))_{i\\in[n]}\\in(\\mathbb{S}^{d-1})^{n}$ is an initial condition which is evolved through the dynamics\nL134: \nL135: (2.3)  |  | $$\\dot{x}_{i}(t)=\\mathbf{P}_{x_{i}(t)}\\left(\\frac{1}{Z_{\\beta,i}(t)}\\sum_{j=1}^{n}e^{\\beta\\langle Q(t)x_{i}(t),K(t)x_{j}(t)\\rangle}V(t)x_{j}(t)\\right)$$  |\nL136: \nL137: for all $i\\in[n]$ and $t\\geq 0$. Here and henceforth\nL138: \nL139:  | $$\\mathbf{P}_{x}y=y-\\langle x,y\\rangle x$$  |\nL140: denotes the projection of $y\\in\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ onto $\\mathrm{T}_{x}\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$. The partition function $Z_{\\beta,i}(t)>0$ reads\nL141: \nL142: (2.4)  |  | $$Z_{\\beta,i}(t)=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}e^{\\beta\\langle Q(t)x_{i}(t),K(t)x_{k}(t)\\rangle}.$$  |\nL143: where $(Q(\\cdot),K(\\cdot),V(\\cdot))$ (standing for Query, Key, and Value) are parameter matrices learned from data, and $\\beta>0$ a fixed number intrinsic to the model^{2}^{2} 2 In practical implementations the inner products are multiplied by $d^{-\\frac{1}{2}}$, which along with the typical magnitude of $Q,K$ leads to the appearance of $\\beta$., which, can be seen as an inverse temperature using terminology from statistical physics. Note that $Q(\\cdot),K(\\cdot)$ need not be square.\nL144: The interacting particle system (cite63†2.3 )–(cite64†2.4 ), a simplified version of which was first written down in [cite65†LLH+20 , cite66†DGCC21 , cite67†SABP22 ], importantly contains the true novelty that Transformers carry with regard to other models: the self-attention mechanism\nL145: \nL146: (2.5)  |  | $$A_{ij}(t):=\\frac{e^{\\langle Q(t)x_{i}(t),K(t)x_{j}(t)\\rangle}}{Z_{\\beta,i}(t)}\\,,\\hskip 28.45274pt(i,j)\\in[n]^{2},$$  |\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSimilarity of Neural Network Representations Revisited (https://proceedings.mlr.press/v97/kornblith19a.html)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v97/kornblith19a.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 50\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2405.13098] Glassy dynamics in deep neural networks: A structural comparison (https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.13098)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.13098\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2505.19458] Recurrent Self-Attention Dynamics: An Energy-Agnostic Perspective from Jacobians (https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.19458)\nciteturn1view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.19458\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 172\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2508.17540] Activation Transport Operators (https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.17540)\nciteturn1view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.17540\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 168\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2402.15390] Explorations of Self-Repair in Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15390)\nciteturn1view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15390\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 168\n"
